        <div id="conductor" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">1. The conductor prompt</h2>
          <p>On 9 October at 10:30 UTC it was a sibling session, not the builder, that noticed sign-in answering 500 after a morning deploy and reported it. A conductor busy building is a conductor not watching production. Rightmark's build doc says the conductor plans, delegates, merges through one integrator and reports, and does not write application code. Dylan's kit puts it in one line, the first line of the prompt below: "You lead. Specialists build. Independent agents check. Scripts hold the plan."</p>
          <p>The ask-first list is Rightmark's, generalized: production deploys, DNS, purchases, emails to real people, pushes, publishing and guardrail files.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_CONDUCTOR} filename="conductor-prompt.md" language="markdown" />
        </div>

        <div id="roster" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">2. The role roster</h2>
          <p>Rightmark ran ten roles, cast like a film crew: conductor, architect, builder, designer, writer, verifier, security, leak-guard, red-team and integrator. Dylan's kit ran eight, each with a model, an effort level and a turn cap. The roster below merges both lists and adds the refuter and sync agent the loop needs.</p>
          <p>Reviewers never fix what they found; a fresh builder does. And every prompt states a tool budget, because an architect and a skeptic that ran past their caps returned nothing at all. The break in the template's first line came on 9 October: a sync agent wrote a stray file into the main checkout and blocked every merge, so sync agents now write nothing there.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_ROSTER} filename="role-roster.md" language="markdown" />
        </div>

        <div id="contract" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">3. The unit contract</h2>
          <p>Rightmark's planning phase produced a 90-unit graph. By 12 October it held 304 units, every one with the same seven keys: id, deps, owns, reads, acceptance, evidence and review. Shared files belong only to integrator units; everyone else asks for a change in their unit report. A wave holds at most 12 units, and no two units in a wave own the same file.</p>
          <p>The break this prevents happened on Dylan's search pages on 10 October: two lanes each invented a "links under a post" design, and the conductor had to unify them. One owner per shared file stops that before it starts.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_CONTRACT} filename="plan/units.json" language="json" />
        </div>

        <div id="planning" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">4. The planning swarm</h2>
          <p>Planning is a swarm too, built to disagree. Rightmark's Phase 0 had three architects draft alone, one optimizing for delivery speed, one for security and privacy, one for SEO and performance. One judge turned the three drafts into one plan, and three critics and a refuter attacked it. Out came 51 decision records, 41 contract files and the 90-unit graph.</p>
          <p>Dylan piloted one lane of Phase 0 before fanning out the rest: 58 facts, 47 confirmed, 11 corrected, 0 refuted, for 297,733 tokens against 260,000 estimated. Decisions run on defaults. Rightmark logged 27 decision rows and 24 dated answers; the swarm used the default until I decided, and open questions waited for the next gate.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_PLANNING} filename="planning-swarm.md" language="markdown" />
        </div>

        <div id="verify" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">5. The verifier and refuter rubric</h2>
          <p>On 6 October Rightmark's scaffold unit burned three hours, three verify rounds and two fix rounds, and came back blocked with zero P0 or P1 findings. The verifier kept inventing new bypasses, and every other unit waited on it. The fix was a severity grade on each unmet criterion, with only P0 and P1 blocking. The unit was accepted the same day, with a hardening unit running beside it.</p>
          <p>Dylan's kit adds two limits. Nothing counts as a blocker or a major unless it cites a gate, a promise or a failing test. After three fix rounds a unit is "stuck" and goes back to the architect, to split it, change the approach or name the decision a person must make. Guard tests must be shown to fail, and that rule paid off: in Dylan's Phase 4, the second review round caught a guard test that could not fail.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_VERIFIER} filename="verifier-rubric.md" language="markdown" />
          <PullQuote>Tests that cannot fail prove nothing.</PullQuote>
        </div>

        <div id="integrate" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">6. The merge lock</h2>
          <p>On 9 October the lock broke under load. A 10-second poll missed every free window for 30 minutes. A gate on a staged merge ran 50 minutes at a load average near 400, and another integrator took the "stale" lock mid-merge. And vitest's 5-second default failed merges at load averages of 40 to 180.</p>
          <p>So the script takes the lock with an atomic mkdir, keeps a heartbeat while it holds it, and breaks a lock only when the heartbeat is old and the owner is gone. It merges with --no-commit, gates the staged merge, and checks it still owns the lock before it commits: on 11 October a 69-minute merge ran without the keeper and lost its lock to another run's integrator. The compose mode exists because two units on Dylan's platform each passed review alone and broke together.</p>
          <p>One rule lives outside the script: never resume a stopped run. On 10 October a resumed run replayed finished reviews, because the resume cache is positional.</p>
          <CopyBlock client:visible text={TEMPLATE_INTEGRATE} filename="scripts/merge-unit.sh" language="bash" />
        </div>

        <div id="watcher" data-section-target class="scroll-mt-24 mt-14">
          <h2 class="font-display text-3xl mb-4">7. The deploy watcher</h2>
          <p>Rightmark went live on 8 October. From then until 12 October, 201 production deploys went live, every one smoke-tested. Two more attempts failed and both were caught: one deploy failed with production unchanged, and on 9 October the watcher's smoke test found /sign-in/ answering 500 and the watcher rolled back on its own. Three failed passes in a row stop the watcher. Each pass deploys a clean export of main, never the working checkout or a peer's edits.</p>
          <p>The smoke list is the sitemap plus a few key pages, never a hand-kept list alone, because the sitemap grows. Dylan's went from 13 pages at go-live to 80 after the search pages.</p>
